#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# random_polygon

#% text
# `random_polygon(k)` gives a random simple polygon with `k` vertices in the unit square; `random_polygon()` one with a random number of vertices.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `random_polygon(type)` and `random_polygon([type, k])` give a polygon of a type: `simple` (the default: random points joined in the order of their angle about their centroid, a star-shaped and so simple polygon), `convex` (a uniformly random convex polygon by the method of Valtr), `star_shaped` (random angles and radii about the centre) or `convex_hull` (the convex hull of random points).

#% item
# `random_polygon([type, dist, k])` draws the points of `convex_hull` and `simple` polygons from the distribution `dist`.

#% item
# `random_polygon(spec, m)` gives a list of `m` polygons, `random_polygon(spec, [m1, m2, ...])` an array of them.

#% item
# `random_polygon(d -> spec)` gives a planar polygon in a random plane through the unit cube of dimension `d`.

#% item
# The draws come from the session's generator: `seed(n)` repeats them.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
seed(1)

#% cell
random_polygon(5)
#% output
#= polygon([[0.2789759153964496, 0.04373894403144052], [0.8968298384314126, 0.07900383719904425], [0.7747576805661607, 0.275631237785959], [0.4219463724596497, 0.6413152138724026], [0.20179147560471555, 0.38170935472141676]])

#% cell
random_polygon([convex, 6])
#% output
#= polygon([[0.6865391610494525, 1.0], [0.3457102831303541, 0.8533607578179228], [0.11272934840922672, 0.743338542448917], [0.015162212564986068, 0.13957446446510155], [0.0, 0.018215028142552936], [0.7823603560924436, 0.0]])

#% cell
length(random_polygon(star_shaped, [2, 3]))
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
graphics(random_polygon([convex_hull, 12]))
#% output
#= image("random_polygon.files/random_polygon-5.svgz")

#% cell
graphics3d(random_polygon(3 -> 8))
#% output
#= image("random_polygon.files/random_polygon-6.svgz")

